{"kind":"request","text":"claude: add a flowchart to docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md so a reader can see the whole process before working through it. The page is 321 lines of prose and shell across seven steps, and someone deciding whether the ceremony is worth it has to read all of it before the shape becomes visible. One diagram near the top answers that in a glance.\n\nUse a mermaid block. GitHub renders mermaid in Markdown natively, so it needs no build step, no committed binary, and no second file to keep in sync with the prose. There is precedent in this repository: docs/reference/architecture.md carries a mermaid flowchart at line 34 with subgraphs and \u003cbr/\u003e line breaks, so the dialect and the style are already established. Follow that page rather than inventing a look. I checked the docset gate before asking: internal/docset/docset.go records each block's lowercased fence info string as Lang, and examples_test.go executes only blocks whose Lang is \"sh\", so a mermaid fence is inert to the gate and will not be run as shell.\n\nWhat the diagram should carry, and it is the reasoning rather than the commands. The artifact-proposal-ratify chain, showing that what gets ratified is the proposal and never the artifact. Adoption sitting before the review request, since that ordering is the point of the design and the page says so in prose that is easy to skim past. The verdict rested on both the artifact and the ratified proposal. The merge consuming the ratified verdict. And the two later shapes as branches off the merged record: revise in place while it is the same decision, replace with a new file naming its predecessor when the decision changes. Who acts at each step -- author, ratifier, reviewer -- matters as much as the order, so show it if the diagram stays readable with it. If it does not, order beats attribution.\n\nKeep it one diagram. If it needs two to stay legible, that is a signal the process is more complicated than the page claims, and I would rather hear that as an assert than have the complexity hidden in a second picture.\n\nYou wrote this page under #9885 and you know what a first-time reader stumbles on, which is why this is yours rather than a general documentation request. Recut on current main, publish an exact-head artifact at docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md, and obtain independent review. The docset gate must stay green.","body":{"conditions":"docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md carries a mermaid flowchart, placed where a reader meets it before the step-by-step, covering the artifact-proposal-ratify chain with the proposal and not the artifact being ratified, adoption preceding the review request, the verdict resting on artifact and ratified proposal, the merge, and the revise and replace branches. Actor attribution per step where it does not cost legibility. One diagram, or an assert explaining why one cannot serve. Follow the mermaid dialect already used in docs/reference/architecture.md. The docset gate stays green. Publish an exact-head artifact at docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md and obtain independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}